#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Un curso gratuito de IBM sobre información cuántica y computación cuántica"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bloch sphere"
msgstr "Esfera de Bloch"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There's a useful geometric way to represent qubit states known as the *Bloch sphere*.\n"
"It's very convenient, but unfortunately it only works for qubits — the analogous representation no longer corresponds to a spherical object once we have three or more classical states of our system.\n"
msgstr ""
"Existe una representación geométrica útil de los estados de qubit conocida como la *esfera de Bloch*.\n"
"Es muy práctica, pero lamentablemente solo funciona para qubits -- la representación análoga ya no corresponde a un objeto esférico una vez que nuestro sistema tiene tres o más estados clásicos.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Qubit states as points on a sphere {#qubit-states-as-points-on-a-sphere}"
msgstr "Estados de qubit como puntos en una esfera {#qubit-states-as-points-on-a-sphere}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's start by thinking about a quantum state vector of a qubit: $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$.\n"
"We can restrict our attention to vectors for which $\\alpha$ is a nonnegative real number because every qubit state vector is equivalent up to a global phase to one for which $\\alpha \\geq 0$.\n"
"This allows us to write\n"
msgstr ""
"Comencemos considerando un vector de estado cuántico de un qubit: $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"Podemos restringir nuestra atención a vectores en los que $\\alpha$ es un número real no negativo, porque todo vector de estado de qubit es equivalente, salvo una fase global, a un vector para el cual $\\alpha \\geq 0.$\n"
"Esto nos permite escribir\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\cos\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i\\phi} \\sin\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\cos\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i\\phi} \\sin\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for two real numbers $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ and $\\phi\\in[0,2\\pi)$.\n"
"Here, we're allowing $\\theta$ to range from $0$ to $\\pi$ and dividing by $2$ in the argument of sine and cosine because this is a conventional way to parameterize vectors of this sort, and it will make things simpler a bit later on.\n"
msgstr ""
"para dos números reales $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ y $\\phi\\in[0,2\\pi)$.\n"
"Aquí dejamos que $\\theta$ varíe de $0$ a $\\pi$ y dividimos por $2$ en el argumento del seno y coseno, ya que esta es una forma convencional de parametrizar vectores de este tipo, lo cual simplifica las cosas un poco más adelante.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, it isn't quite the case that the numbers $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ are uniquely determined by a given quantum state vector $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$, but it is nearly so.\n"
"In particular, if $\\beta = 0$, then $\\theta = 0$ and it doesn't make any difference what value $\\phi$ takes, so it can be chosen arbitrarily.\n"
"Similarly, if $\\alpha = 0$, then $\\theta = \\pi$, and once again $\\phi$ is irrelevant (as our state is equivalent to $e^{i\\phi}\\vert 1\\rangle$ for any $\\phi$ up to a global phase).\n"
"If, however, neither $\\alpha$ nor $\\beta$ is zero, then there's a unique choice for the pair $(\\theta,\\phi)$ for which $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ is equivalent to $\\alpha\\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta\\vert 1\\rangle$ up to a global phase.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ahora bien, los números $\\theta$ y $\\phi$ no están exactamente determinados de forma única por un vector de estado cuántico $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$ dado, pero casi.\n"
"En particular, si $\\beta = 0$, entonces $\\theta = 0$, y no importa que valor tome $\\phi$, por lo que puede elegirse arbitrariamente.\n"
"De igual manera, si $\\alpha = 0$, entonces $\\theta = \\pi$, y nuevamente $\\phi$ es irrelevante (ya que nuestro estado es equivalente a $e^{i\\phi}\\vert 1\\rangle$ salvo una fase global para cualquier $\\phi$).\n"
"Sin embargo, si ni $\\alpha$ ni $\\beta$ son cero, entonces hay una elección única del par $(\\theta,\\phi)$ para el cual $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ es equivalente a $\\alpha\\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta\\vert 1\\rangle$ salvo una fase global.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, let's consider the density matrix representation of this state.\n"
msgstr "Consideremos ahora la representación como matriz de densidad de este estado.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) & e^{-i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"e^{i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2) & \\sin^2(\\theta/2)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) & e^{-i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"e^{i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2) & \\sin^2(\\theta/2)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use some trigonometric identities,\n"
msgstr "Podemos utilizar algunas identidades trigonométricas,\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 + \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 - \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\theta/2) \\sin(\\theta/2) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2},\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 + \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 - \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\theta/2) \\sin(\\theta/2) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2},\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "as well as the formula $e^{i\\phi} = \\cos(\\phi) + i\\sin(\\phi)$, to simplify the density matrix as follows.\n"
msgstr "así como la fórmula $e^{i\\phi} = \\cos(\\phi) + i\\sin(\\phi)$, para simplificar la matriz de densidad de la siguiente manera.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 + \\cos(\\theta) & (\\cos(\\phi) - i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta)\\\\[1mm]\n"
"(\\cos(\\phi) + i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta) & 1 - \\cos(\\theta)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 + \\cos(\\theta) & (\\cos(\\phi) - i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta)\\\\[1mm]\n"
"(\\cos(\\phi) + i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta) & 1 - \\cos(\\theta)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This makes it easy to express this density matrix as a linear combination of the Pauli matrices:\n"
msgstr "Esto facilita expresar esta matriz de densidad como combinación lineal de las matrices de Pauli:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & 1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_x =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    1 & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_y =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & -i\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    i & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_z =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & 1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_x =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    1 & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_y =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & -i\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    i & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_z =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specifically, we conclude that\n"
msgstr "Concretamente, obtenemos\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi)\\sigma_x + \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi) \\sigma_y + \\cos(\\theta) \\sigma_z}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi)\\sigma_x + \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi) \\sigma_y + \\cos(\\theta) \\sigma_z}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The coefficients of $\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_y$, and $\\sigma_z$ in the numerator of this expression are all real numbers, so we can collect them together to form a vector in an ordinary, three-dimensional Euclidean space.\n"
msgstr "Los coeficientes de $\\sigma_x,$ $\\sigma_y$ y $\\sigma_z$ en el numerador de esta expresión son todos números reales, por lo que podemos agruparlos en un vector en un espacio euclidiano tridimensional ordinario.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl(\\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi), \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi), \\cos(\\theta)\\bigr)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl(\\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi), \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi), \\cos(\\theta)\\bigr)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In fact, this is a unit vector.\n"
"Using *spherical coordinates* it can be written as $(1,\\theta,\\phi)$.\n"
"The first coordinate, $1$, represents the *radius* or *radial distance* (which is always $1$ in this case), $\\theta$ represents the *polar angle*, and $\\phi$ represents the *azimuthal angle*.\n"
msgstr ""
"De hecho, este es un vector unitario.\n"
"En *coordenadas esféricas* puede escribirse como $(1,\\theta,\\phi).$\n"
"La primera coordenada, $1,$ representa el *radio* o *distancia radial* (que en este caso siempre es $1$), $\\theta$ representa el *ángulo polar* y $\\phi$ el *ángulo azimutal*.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In words, thinking about a sphere as the planet Earth, the polar angle $\\theta$ is how far we rotate south from the north pole to reach the point being described, from $0$ to $\\pi = 180^{\\circ}$, while the azimuthal angle $\\phi$ is how far we rotate east from the prime meridian, from $0$ to $2\\pi = 360^{\\circ}$.\n"
"This assumes that we define the prime meridian to be the curve on the surface of the sphere from one pole to the other that passes through the positive $x$-axis.\n"
msgstr ""
"Si imaginamos la esfera como la Tierra, el ángulo polar $\\theta$ es la rotación hacia el sur desde el polo norte para llegar al punto descrito, de $0$ a $\\pi = 180°$, mientras que el ángulo azimutal $\\phi$ indica la rotación hacia el este desde el meridiano de referencia, de $0$ a $2\\pi = 360°$.\n"
"El meridiano de referencia se define como la curva sobre la superficie de la esfera que va de un polo al otro a lo largo del eje $x$ positivo.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of a point on the unit 2-sphere in terms of its spherical coordinates.](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/spherical-coordinates.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustración de un punto en la 2-esfera unitaria en coordenadas esféricas.](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/spherical-coordinates.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every point on the sphere can be described in this way — which is to say that the points we obtain when we range over all possible pure states of a qubit correspond precisely to a sphere in $3$ real dimensions.\n"
"(This sphere is typically called the *unit $2$-sphere* because the surface of this sphere is two-dimensional.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Todo punto de la esfera puede describirse de esta manera — lo que significa que los puntos que obtenemos al recorrer todos los posibles estados puros de un qubit corresponden precisamente a una esfera en $3$ dimensiones reales.\n"
"(Esta esfera se llama típicamente la *esfera unitaria $2$* porque la superficie de esta esfera es bidimensional.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When we associate points on the unit $2$-sphere with pure states of qubits, we obtain the *Bloch sphere* representation these states.\n"
msgstr "Cuando asociamos puntos en la esfera unitaria $2$ con estados puros de qubits, obtenemos la representación de estos estados llamada *esfera de Bloch*.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Six important examples {#six-important-examples}"
msgstr "Seis ejemplos importantes {#six-important-examples}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The standard basis* $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Let's start with the state $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"
"As a density matrix it can be written like this.\n"
msgstr ""
"*La base estándar* $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Comencemos con el estado $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"
"Como matriz de densidad, puede escribirse así.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0 \\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0 \\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   By collecting the coefficients of the Pauli matrices in the numerator, we see that the corresponding point on the unit $2$-sphere using Cartesian coordinates is $(0,0,1)$.\n"
"   In spherical coordinates this point is $(1,0,\\phi)$, where $\\phi$ can be any angle.\n"
"   This is consistent with the expression\n"
msgstr ""
"   Recogiendo los coeficientes de las matrices de Pauli en el numerador, vemos que el punto correspondiente en la 2-esfera unitaria en coordenadas cartesianas es $(0,0,1)$.\n"
"   En coordenadas esféricas, este punto es $(1,0,\\phi)$, donde $\\phi$ puede ser cualquier ángulo.\n"
"   Esto es consistente con la expresión\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0\\rangle = \\cos(0) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i \\phi} \\sin(0) \\vert 1\\rangle,\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0\\rangle = \\cos(0) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i \\phi} \\sin(0) \\vert 1\\rangle,\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   which also works for any $\\phi$.\n"
"   Intuitively speaking, the polar angle $\\theta$ is zero, so we're at the north pole of the Bloch sphere, where the azimuthal angle is irrelevant.\n"
msgstr ""
"   que también es válida para cualquier $\\phi$.\n"
"   Intuitivamente: el angulo polar $\\theta$ es cero, así que estamos en el polo norte de la esfera de Bloch, donde el angulo azimutal es irrelevante.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   Along similar lines, the density matrix for the state $\\vert 1\\rangle$ can be written like so.\n"
msgstr "   Correspondientemente, la matriz de densidad para el estado $\\vert 1\\rangle$ puede escribirse así.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 1 \\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 1 \\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   This time the Cartesian coordinates are $(0,0,-1)$. In spherical coordinates this point is $(1,\\pi,\\phi)$ where $\\phi$ can be any angle. In this case the polar angle is all the way to $\\pi$, so we're at the south pole where the azimuthal angle is again irrelevant.\n"
msgstr "   Esta vez las coordenadas cartesianas son $(0,0,-1)$. En coordenadas esfericas, este punto es $(1,\\pi,\\phi)$, donde $\\phi$ puede ser cualquier angulo. En este caso el angulo polar es exactamente $\\pi$, así que estamos en el polo sur, donde el angulo azimutal es nuevamente irrelevante.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The basis $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$.*\n"
"We have these expressions for the density matrices corresponding to these states.\n"
msgstr ""
"*La base $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$.*\n"
"Para las matrices de densidad de estos estados se cumplen las siguientes expresiones.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_x}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_x}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_x}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_x}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   The corresponding points on the unit $2$-sphere have Cartesian coordinates $(1,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$,\n"
"   and spherical coordinates $(1,\\pi/2,0)$ and $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi)$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""
"   Los puntos correspondientes en la 2-esfera unitaria tienen coordenadas cartesianas $(1,0,0)$ y $(-1,0,0)$,\n"
"   y coordenadas esfericas $(1,\\pi/2,0)$ y $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi)$ respectivamente.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   In words, $\\vert +\\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the positive $x$-axis intersects the unit $2$-sphere and $\\vert -\\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the negative $x$-axis intersects it. More intuitively, $\\vert +\\rangle$ is on the equator of the Bloch sphere where it meets the prime meridian, and $\\vert - \\rangle$ is on the equator on the opposite side of the sphere.\n"
msgstr "   En otras palabras: $\\vert +\\rangle$ corresponde al punto donde el eje $x$ positivo intersecta la 2-esfera unitaria, y $\\vert -\\rangle$ corresponde al punto donde el eje $x$ negativo la intersecta. Dicho de forma más intuitiva: $\\vert +\\rangle$ se encuentra en el ecuador de la esfera de Bloch, donde este intersecta el meridiano de referencia, y $\\vert - \\rangle$ se encuentra en el ecuador en el lado opuesto de la esfera.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The basis* $\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\}$.\n"
"As we saw earlier in the lesson, these two states are defined like this:\n"
msgstr ""
"*La base* $\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Como vimos al comienzo de la lección, estos dos estados se definen de la siguiente manera:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle.\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle.\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   This time we have these expressions.\n"
msgstr "   Esta vez obtenemos las siguientes expresiones.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_y}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_y}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_y}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_y}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   The corresponding points on the unit $2$-sphere have Cartesian coordinates $(0,1,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$,\n"
"   and spherical coordinates $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ and $(1,\\pi/2,3\\pi/2)$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""
"   Los puntos correspondientes en la 2-esfera unitaria tienen coordenadas cartesianas $(0,1,0)$ y $(0,-1,0)$,\n"
"   y coordenadas esfericas $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ y $(1,\\pi/2,3\\pi/2)$ respectivamente.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   In words, $\\vert {+i} \\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the positive $y$-axis intersects the unit $2$-sphere and $\\vert {-i} \\rangle$ to the point where the negative $y$-axis intersects it.\n"
msgstr "   En otras palabras: $\\vert {+i} \\rangle$ corresponde al punto donde el eje $y$ positivo intersecta la 2-esfera unitaria, y $\\vert {-i} \\rangle$ al punto donde el eje $y$ negativo la intersecta.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of six examples of pure states on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/six-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustración de seis ejemplos de estados puros en la esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/six-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's another class of quantum state vectors that has appeared from time to time throughout this series, including previously in this lesson.\n"
msgstr "Aquí hay otra clase de vectores de estado cuántico que aparece repetidamente a lo largo de esta serie, incluyendo anteriormente en esta lección.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle = \\cos(\\alpha) \\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\alpha) \\vert 1\\rangle \\qquad \\text{(for $\\alpha \\in [0,\\pi)$)}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle = \\cos(\\alpha) \\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\alpha) \\vert 1\\rangle \\qquad \\text{(para $\\alpha \\in [0,\\pi)$)}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The density matrix representation of each of these states is as follows.\n"
msgstr "La representación como matriz de densidad de cada uno de estos estados es la siguiente.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\alpha} \\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\alpha) & \\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha) & \\sin^2(\\alpha)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(2\\alpha) \\sigma_x + \\cos(2\\alpha) \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\alpha} \\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\alpha) & \\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha) & \\sin^2(\\alpha)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(2\\alpha) \\sigma_x + \\cos(2\\alpha) \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following figure illustrates the corresponding points on the Bloch sphere for a few choices for $\\alpha$.\n"
msgstr "La siguiente figura ilustra los puntos correspondientes en la esfera de Bloch para algunas elecciones de $\\alpha$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of real-valued qubit state vectors on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/real-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustración de vectores de estado de qubit con valores reales en la esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/real-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Convex combinations of points {#convex-combinations-of-points}"
msgstr "Combinaciones convexas de puntos {#convex-combinations-of-points}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Similar to what we have already discussed for density matrices, we can take convex combinations of points on the Bloch sphere to obtain representations of qubit density matrices.\n"
"In general, this results in points *inside* of the Bloch sphere, which represent density matrices of states that are not pure.\n"
"Sometimes we refer to the *Bloch ball* when we wish to be explicit about the inclusion of points inside of the Bloch sphere as representations of qubit density matrices.\n"
msgstr ""
"De manera similar a lo discutido para las matrices de densidad, podemos formar combinaciones convexas de puntos en la esfera de Bloch para obtener representaciones de matrices de densidad de qubit.\n"
"En general, esto produce puntos *dentro* de la esfera de Bloch, que representan matrices de densidad de estados que no son puros.\n"
"A veces se habla de la *bola de Bloch* cuando se desea indicar explícitamente que también se incluyen puntos en el interior de la esfera de Bloch como representaciones de matrices de densidad de qubit.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example, we've seen that the density matrix $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{I}$, which represents the completely mixed state of a qubit, can be written in these two alternative ways:\n"
msgstr "Por ejemplo, hemos visto que la matriz de densidad $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{I}$, que representa el estado completamente mezclado de un qubit, puede escribirse de estas dos formas alternativas:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{y}\\quad\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We also have\n"
msgstr "Ademas, se cumple que\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert {+i}\\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2} \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert {+i}\\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2} \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"and more generally we can use any two orthogonal qubit state vectors (which will always correspond to two antipodal points on the Bloch sphere).\n"
"If we average the corresponding points on the Bloch sphere in a similar way, we obtain the same point, which in this case is at the center of the sphere.\n"
"This is consistent with the observation that\n"
msgstr ""
"y más generalmente podemos utilizar cualesquiera dos vectores de estado de qubit ortogonales (que siempre corresponden a dos puntos antipodales en la esfera de Bloch).\n"
"Si promediamos los puntos correspondientes en la esfera de Bloch de manera similar, obtenemos el mismo punto, que en este caso se encuentra en el centro de la esfera.\n"
"Esto es consistente con la observación de que\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_x + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_y + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_z}{2},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_x + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_y + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_z}{2},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "giving us the Cartesian coordinates $(0,0,0)$.\n"
msgstr "lo que nos da las coordenadas cartesianas $(0,0,0)$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A different example concerning convex combinations of Bloch sphere points is the one discussed in the previous subsection.\n"
msgstr "Otro ejemplo de combinaciones convexas de puntos de la esfera de Bloch es el discutido en el apartado anterior.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following figure illustrates these two different ways of obtaining this density matrix as a convex combination of pure states.\n"
msgstr "La siguiente figura ilustra estas dos formas distintas de obtener esta matriz de densidad como combinación convexa de estados puros.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of the average of the zero state and the plus state on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/Bloch-sphere-zero-plus-average.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustración del promedio del estado cero y el estado más en la esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/Bloch-sphere-zero-plus-average.avif)\n"
